WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.390
Please give a warm welcome to Matthew Dix.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:09.210
It's 1991 and I'm 20 years old and

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.250
I'm driving home from the very first

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:14.250
booty qual of my entire life, and

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.470
I am excited because I don't know.

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:19.600
That this is going to also be the very

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.240
last booty call of my entire life.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:25.060
And then it's in that moment of excitement

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.480
that the right front tire of my 1976 Chevy

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.880
Malibu blows out, but it doesn't deflate.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.080
It disintegrates.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.300
It throws rubber rubber on the road in

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.160
a way that I didn't think was possible,

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.680
and it takes everything I can to get the

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:41.500
car over to the shoulder and to a stop.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:43.240
And it's 1991.

00:00:43.605 --> 00:00:44.805
I don't have a cell phone,

00:00:45.105 --> 00:00:46.335
so I do the only thing I can.

00:00:46.335 --> 00:00:47.715
I start hiking up the road to look

00:00:47.715 --> 00:00:51.165
for help seven hours later after

00:00:51.165 --> 00:00:52.995
giving all of my money to a half

00:00:52.995 --> 00:00:54.735
naked mountain man named Winston.

00:00:55.125 --> 00:00:56.895
I have a bald tire on my

00:00:56.895 --> 00:00:58.395
car and I'm heading home.

00:00:58.665 --> 00:00:59.775
When I look at the instrument

00:00:59.775 --> 00:01:02.055
panel and I see that I have no gas

00:01:02.445 --> 00:01:04.010
and all of the money that I have.

00:01:04.474 --> 00:01:07.655
Is in Winston's hand, and it's 1991.

00:01:07.655 --> 00:01:08.854
I don't have a credit card.

00:01:08.884 --> 00:01:10.205
I don't have an ATM card.

00:01:10.445 --> 00:01:12.455
I don't even have a checking account.

00:01:13.264 --> 00:01:15.335
And so I take the next exit and I roll

00:01:15.335 --> 00:01:17.945
my car into a Getty station, and as I

00:01:17.945 --> 00:01:21.035
park the car, I'm angry and I'm sad.

00:01:21.604 --> 00:01:22.955
I'm 20 years old.

00:01:23.345 --> 00:01:24.845
I'm a McDonald's manager.

00:01:25.205 --> 00:01:27.755
I make $7 and 25 cents an hour,

00:01:27.994 --> 00:01:29.945
and I'm the richest person I know.

00:01:30.785 --> 00:01:31.955
My mother is living on

00:01:31.955 --> 00:01:32.880
welfare with my sister.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.000
My father's left 10 years ago, and

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.979
the only friend that I have who even

00:01:37.979 --> 00:01:40.289
has a credit card or a car that can

00:01:40.289 --> 00:01:42.000
make this a hundred mile trip to New

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.370
Hampshire is off on some college weekend,

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:45.690
and I can't get in touch with him.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:48.270
And this was not the plan for my life.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.370
I was not supposed to be this alone.

00:01:51.164 --> 00:01:52.274
And so I go into the Getty

00:01:52.274 --> 00:01:54.074
Station to beg for gas.

00:01:54.434 --> 00:01:56.925
$8 worth of gas is all I need 'cause

00:01:56.925 --> 00:02:00.734
it's 1991 and gas is 85 cents a gallon.

00:02:01.514 --> 00:02:03.164
And the kid behind the counter

00:02:03.225 --> 00:02:04.335
will not give it to me.

00:02:04.725 --> 00:02:06.225
He doesn't wanna lose his job and

00:02:06.225 --> 00:02:08.324
I offer everything and he says no.

00:02:08.685 --> 00:02:10.185
So I go back to my car and my plan

00:02:10.185 --> 00:02:11.894
is to wait for the next shift change.

00:02:12.135 --> 00:02:13.635
Maybe someone more sympathetic will

00:02:13.635 --> 00:02:15.915
take over, but as I climb into my car,

00:02:15.915 --> 00:02:17.924
I see my McDonald's uniform crumpled

00:02:17.924 --> 00:02:19.875
in the back, and I have an idea.

00:02:20.695 --> 00:02:22.075
An hour later, I'm in a

00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:23.365
residential neighborhood about

00:02:23.365 --> 00:02:24.775
a mile from the Getty Station.

00:02:25.165 --> 00:02:26.965
I'm on the porch of a red brick

00:02:26.965 --> 00:02:29.095
house, and I'm knocking on the door.

00:02:29.335 --> 00:02:31.075
I'm wearing my McDonald's uniform,

00:02:31.075 --> 00:02:32.485
the blue shirt, the blue pants,

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:35.485
the blue tie, the door opens, and

00:02:35.485 --> 00:02:36.985
there's a man standing in front of me.

00:02:36.985 --> 00:02:39.085
And I know at that moment that this

00:02:39.115 --> 00:02:42.535
is a bad idea, but it's three o'clock

00:02:42.535 --> 00:02:44.635
on a Sunday afternoon, and I'm on his

00:02:44.635 --> 00:02:46.915
porch in a McDonald's uniform, and the

00:02:46.915 --> 00:02:49.105
only way to get off is to go forward.

00:02:49.505 --> 00:02:52.715
So I say to him, my name is Matt.

00:02:53.135 --> 00:02:54.860
I'm collecting money for the Ronald

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:56.645
McDonald Children's Charities.

00:02:57.485 --> 00:02:59.465
Can you please make a donation?

00:03:00.215 --> 00:03:02.075
And this man stares at me like

00:03:02.075 --> 00:03:04.415
Stone Hen she does not move.

00:03:04.475 --> 00:03:07.115
He's 50, but he might as well be 500.

00:03:07.115 --> 00:03:09.185
He is all of the wisdom and knowledge

00:03:09.185 --> 00:03:11.075
of the world wrapped into one man.

00:03:11.075 --> 00:03:12.785
And I know he knows I'm lying.

00:03:13.055 --> 00:03:14.345
And the next words that come out

00:03:14.345 --> 00:03:17.195
of my mouth, I don't expect, I say.

00:03:17.435 --> 00:03:20.075
My mom died of cancer when I was young,

00:03:21.395 --> 00:03:24.155
and my sister has cancer now, and I'm

00:03:24.155 --> 00:03:27.425
just trying to do what I can to help.

00:03:27.455 --> 00:03:29.345
And he looks at me and points

00:03:29.345 --> 00:03:31.355
and says, you stay right there.

00:03:31.625 --> 00:03:33.485
And he goes back into the house and closes

00:03:33.485 --> 00:03:35.915
the door, and I know that he's calling

00:03:35.915 --> 00:03:38.195
the police, and I'm going to get arrested

00:03:38.195 --> 00:03:39.695
for stealing money from McDonald's,

00:03:39.695 --> 00:03:41.315
which ironically, two years later

00:03:41.315 --> 00:03:45.575
will actually happen, but not on this.

00:03:45.595 --> 00:03:48.805
Day because he comes back to the door

00:03:48.955 --> 00:03:51.205
and he has $20 in his hand, which

00:03:51.205 --> 00:03:53.665
is like $20,000 to me on this day.

00:03:53.845 --> 00:03:55.465
And he presses it into my palm

00:03:55.465 --> 00:03:57.175
and I say, no, that's too much.

00:03:57.385 --> 00:03:59.755
And he says to me, my wife

00:03:59.755 --> 00:04:01.735
died of cancer five years ago,

00:04:03.204 --> 00:04:05.605
and I think this is a really.

00:04:06.214 --> 00:04:07.744
Bad idea I had.

00:04:08.015 --> 00:04:09.964
And before I know it, I am sitting

00:04:09.964 --> 00:04:12.035
on the porch with this man and he

00:04:12.035 --> 00:04:13.535
asks me about, my mother died of,

00:04:13.535 --> 00:04:14.975
and I say breast cancer, 'cause it's

00:04:14.975 --> 00:04:16.685
the first one that pops to my mind.

00:04:16.985 --> 00:04:18.394
And he says, what does your sister have?

00:04:18.394 --> 00:04:19.445
And I say, leukemia, which

00:04:19.445 --> 00:04:20.584
I don't actually know is a

00:04:20.584 --> 00:04:22.445
cancer, but I know kids get it.

00:04:22.925 --> 00:04:24.455
And he tells me that his wife

00:04:24.455 --> 00:04:25.865
Lisa, died of lung cancer.

00:04:26.305 --> 00:04:27.775
And that his kids live on the West

00:04:27.775 --> 00:04:31.015
Coast and he tells me how she struggled

00:04:31.135 --> 00:04:33.655
for two years and how he wanted to

00:04:33.655 --> 00:04:35.844
tell her it was okay to go, but he

00:04:35.844 --> 00:04:38.635
couldn't, and how badly he felt that

00:04:38.635 --> 00:04:40.615
he watched her struggle for so long.

00:04:41.215 --> 00:04:42.985
And I sat on that porch and I

00:04:42.985 --> 00:04:44.425
listened to him and I prayed.

00:04:44.425 --> 00:04:45.865
He wouldn't ask me anything

00:04:46.225 --> 00:04:47.844
and I just wanted to get off.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.130
And so finally we were hugging and

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:52.230
I was walking down the walk with $20

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:54.210
in my hand and it felt like poison.

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:57.540
I got to my car and I sat in there and

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:00.360
I remembered how lonely I felt, and

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:03.480
then I felt so stupid and small and.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:05.900
Because I knew at that moment I

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:08.300
didn't understand real loneliness.

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:10.970
I was a 20-year-old kid with friends

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:13.430
and I was stuck in New Hampshire,

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:15.800
but I had walked away from a man

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:18.020
who was in a tiny house with a wife

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:19.760
that was gone and the kids that were

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:22.100
gone, and he had a loneliness in

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:24.200
him that I would never understand.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:26.510
And so I put the gas in

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:28.310
my car and I drove home.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:33.080
But he has always been with me.

00:05:34.425 --> 00:05:37.125
I have never left that man on that porch

00:05:37.125 --> 00:05:39.705
that day, and I'm hopeful that I'll

00:05:39.705 --> 00:05:42.135
never be as lonely as he was that day.

00:05:42.705 --> 00:05:43.030
Thank you.

